-- certifications_procurement.sql
-- Why: the founder asked that every position be tested against the top certifications for its
-- profession. This file authors the FIVE procurement question banks (40 questions each, real exam
-- difficulty, scenario and application) that the marketplace and supply seats sit:
--   CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply
--   CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply
--   MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)
--   CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)
--   CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)
-- Syllabus areas are named as the awarding body names them (CIPS module codes, the three ISM CPSM
-- exams, the three ASCM CSCP domains). Each bank is guarded by a not-exists on its certification so
-- a bank is authored once even if two authors share a certification; the first author wins.
-- role_certification rows and the sitter are written by a sibling file, not here.
-- Apply: node scripts/apply_sql.mjs scripts/sql/certifications_procurement.sql --apply --ddl

insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select v.* from (values
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M2 Defining Business Needs','exam','A buyer must specify 400 office chairs. The market contains many competing designs that all achieve the same seating outcome. Which specification approach is most likely to widen the bidder pool and reduce price?','A detailed conformance specification listing every dimension, weld and fabric','An output or performance specification stating the outcome and the measurable requirements','A brand-name specification naming the incumbent manufacturer','A sample-based specification requiring an exact physical match to the current chair','B','A performance specification states what the item must achieve and leaves the design to the market, so more suppliers can bid. Conformance, brand and sample specifications all narrow the field to those who can copy an existing design.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M2 Defining Business Needs','hard','The user insists the specification must not block innovation but the chair must still be certified to carry 120 kg. What should the buyer write?','Remove the load rating and invite suppliers to propose a rating','Name the incumbent product and allow unspecified equivalents','A conformance specification covering frame, welds and fabric','A performance specification in which the 120 kg load is stated as a measurable, testable requirement','D','A performance specification can carry hard, testable constraints without dictating design. Removing the rating loses control of a safety requirement, and naming a product without defining equivalence hands the decision to the bidder.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M3 Commercial Contracting','exam','A buyer sends a purchase order on its own terms. The supplier acknowledges on its own terms, and the buyer accepts and uses the delivery without comment. Whose terms usually govern?','The supplier terms, as the last set put forward before performance','The buyer terms, because the purchase order came first','Neither, because no contract has formed','Both sets apply equally and the conflict is split','A','In the battle of the forms the last set of terms sent before performance is treated as the offer, and taking delivery is conduct amounting to acceptance. The buyer loses its own terms by performing without objecting.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M3 Commercial Contracting','exam','Which combination is essential for a simple contract to come into existence?','A signed written document held by both parties','Registration with a chamber of commerce','Offer, acceptance, consideration and an intention to create legal relations','A performance bond covering the contract value','C','Those four elements form a simple contract; writing, registration and bonds are evidential or commercial extras. A contract can exist without any of them provided the four elements are present.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M1 Scope and Influence of Procurement and Supply','exam','A finance director measures procurement value only as the reduction in unit price. What is the strongest argument for a broader measure?','Price alone ignores acquisition, holding, quality failure and disposal costs that the business pays later','Price data is difficult to audit reliably','Suppliers dislike being compared on price','Savings should be reported monthly rather than annually','A','Unit price is one element of the cost the business actually incurs across the life of the purchase. A measure that excludes the other elements rewards decisions that move cost rather than remove it.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M5 Commercial Negotiation','hard','A buyer holds three comparable quotations and a fixed deadline. The lowest bidder has confirmed it cannot meet the date. What is the strongest negotiating position?','Disclose the deadline pressure to the second bidder to build goodwill','Award to the lowest bidder and press for an early delivery','Negotiate with the second bidder using the lowest quotation as the price benchmark while holding the date as the decisive variable','Cancel and re-tender the requirement to reset the market','C','The lowest bid is still useful as a benchmark even though its supplier cannot perform, and the date is the variable that decides the award. Revealing the pressure weakens the buyer, and re-tendering spends the very time that is scarce.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M6 Supplier Relationships','exam','A stationery supplier accounts for 0.4 per cent of spend, is easily replaced and carries no continuity risk. Which relationship type is appropriate?','A strategic partnership with joint board level reviews','Single sourcing supported by open-book cost data','A co-development agreement with shared intellectual property','A transactional arms-length arrangement through a catalogue or call-off framework','D','Relationship intensity should follow value and risk, and this category carries neither. Investing partnership effort here consumes management time that strategic categories need.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M4 Ethical and Responsible Sourcing','exam','During evaluation, a bidder offers the lead buyer a personal weekend trip. What is the correct action?','Accept the offer and declare it after the award is made','Decline it, record the offer in the gifts and hospitality register and inform the line manager','Accept it on behalf of the company rather than personally','Ignore the offer because no decision has yet been taken','B','Declining and recording protects both the individual and the integrity of the award, and the record is what an audit later relies on. Accepting in any form, or staying silent, leaves the decision open to challenge.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M2 Defining Business Needs','exam','Three quotations arrive for the same goods: AED 100 ex works Shanghai, AED 118 CIF Jebel Ali and AED 125 delivered duty paid to a Dubai warehouse. How should they be compared?','Compare the headline figures as submitted','Award to the ex works quotation because it carries the lowest number','Normalise all three to the same delivery point and duty position before comparing','Award to the delivered duty paid quotation because it carries the least paperwork','C','The three prices buy different scopes of carriage, risk and duty, so the headline figures are not comparable. Normalising to a common delivery point is the only way to see which supplier is actually cheapest.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M7 Whole Life Asset Management','exam','A five year printer fleet contract is being evaluated. Which cost element is most likely to dominate the whole life cost?','Consumables and maintenance across the service life','The purchase price of the machines','The delivery cost of the machines','The cost of disposal at end of life','A','For consumable-driven assets the running cost repeats every month for years, while purchase, delivery and disposal happen once. Evaluating on purchase price alone reliably picks the more expensive machine.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M7 Whole Life Asset Management','hard','Machine X costs AED 90,000 to buy and AED 12,000 a year to run. Machine Y costs AED 60,000 to buy and AED 20,000 a year to run. Both last five years. Ignoring discounting, which is cheaper over the life and by how much?','Machine Y, by AED 30,000','Machine X, by AED 10,000','Machine X, by AED 30,000','Machine Y, by AED 10,000','B','Machine X totals 90,000 plus 60,000, which is AED 150,000; machine Y totals 60,000 plus 100,000, which is AED 160,000. The cheaper purchase is the dearer asset once five years of running cost are counted.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M8 Procurement and Supply in Practice','exam','Which document formally invites priced offers against a defined specification for a repeat, well understood requirement?','A prior information notice','A request for information','An expression of interest','A request for quotation','D','A request for quotation asks for prices against a specification that is already settled. The other three gather market interest or capability information and do not produce comparable prices.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M6 Supplier Relationships','exam','A component sits in the Kraljic bottleneck quadrant: low spend value but high supply risk. What is the appropriate strategy?','Leverage the spend through competitive bidding','Build a strategic partnership with joint investment','Consolidate the volume and run a tender','Secure continuity of supply, hold buffer stock and qualify alternative sources','D','Bottleneck items give the buyer little commercial leverage but can stop the business, so the objective is continuity rather than price. Tendering a small spend in a thin market does not change the underlying risk.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M6 Supplier Relationships','hard','A category is high in value but low in supply risk, with many capable suppliers. Which lever gives the buyer most?','A long term partnership with open-book costing','Competitive tendering and spend consolidation that exploit the buyer purchasing power','Buffer stocking against supply interruption','Vendor managed inventory at each site','B','Leverage items reward the use of purchasing power because alternatives are genuinely available. Partnership, buffer stock and vendor managed inventory address risks that this category does not carry.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M3 Commercial Contracting','exam','When is a liquidated damages clause most likely to be enforceable?','When it is set high enough to deter the supplier from breaching','When it equals the buyer lost profit however large that turns out to be','When it is a genuine pre-estimate of the loss made at the time of contracting','When it is agreed between the parties after the breach has occurred','C','Liquidated damages must be a genuine pre-estimate of loss rather than a penalty designed to deter. A figure set to punish, or set after the event, is open to challenge.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M5 Commercial Negotiation','exam','What gives a buyer the most durable source of power in a commercial negotiation?','A credible and prepared alternative to a negotiated agreement','A deliberately extreme opening demand','A tight internal deadline that forces a decision','A long history of trading with the supplier','A','Power in negotiation comes from being able to walk away to a real alternative that has been prepared and costed. Opening demands, deadlines and history do not change what happens if no deal is struck.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M4 Ethical and Responsible Sourcing','exam','A media report alleges forced labour at a factory that supplies one of the buyer tier one suppliers. What is the correct first step?','Map the supply chain beyond tier one and assess risk by country and process','Terminate the tier one supplier immediately','Ask the tier one supplier to sign a code of conduct and close the matter','Issue a public statement denying any connection','A','Nothing can be remediated until the buyer knows which sites are actually in the chain and where the risk sits. Immediate termination removes visibility and leaves the workers worse off, and a signature is not evidence.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M1 Scope and Influence of Procurement and Supply','hard','Procurement is asked to add value in a category of low value, high frequency orders. Where is the largest gain?','Negotiating each order individually with the supplier','Adding an approval step above a low value threshold','Requiring three quotations for every order','Removing transaction cost through a call-off framework and catalogue self service','D','In high frequency, low value categories the process cost per order can exceed the saving available on price. Adding quotations and approvals increases the cost that dominates the category.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M2 Defining Business Needs','exam','A budget holder writes a specification that names one manufacturer and one model. What is the principal risk?','The specification is too vague to evaluate','Competition is restricted, the buyer loses price leverage and the award may breach procurement policy','Delivery will take longer than usual','Quality will be lower than the market average','B','Naming a product removes the competitive tension that produces the price, and most policies require justification for restricting the field. The specification is not vague, it is too narrow.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M3 Commercial Contracting','exam','Under the UAE Civil Transactions Law, an offer made during a meeting that is not accepted before the meeting ends will normally have what effect?','It remains open for thirty days from the date it was made','It converts automatically into a binding contract','It lapses, unless the offeror fixed a period for acceptance','It must be accepted in writing within seven days','C','An offer made in a session lapses with that session unless the offeror has allowed a period for acceptance. Silence at the end of the meeting is not acceptance and creates no contract.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M3 Commercial Contracting','exam','Goods are bought CIF Jebel Ali under Incoterms 2020. Where does risk pass from seller to buyer?','On arrival of the vessel at Jebel Ali','When the goods clear UAE customs','When the goods are loaded on board the vessel at the port of shipment','On delivery to the buyer warehouse in Dubai','C','Under CIF the seller pays carriage and insurance to the named destination, but risk transfers on loading at the port of shipment. Cost and risk transfer at different points, which is what most buyers misread.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M3 Commercial Contracting','hard','A UAE principal appoints a distributor and the arrangement is registered as a commercial agency. What is the main consequence for the principal?','The agency may be terminated at will on seven days notice','The registered agent gains statutory protection, and termination or non-renewal without valid cause can attract compensation','The agent must be a foreign owned company','The agreement is void unless it is written in English','B','Registration under the UAE commercial agencies regime gives the agent protected status, and ending the relationship without a valid reason exposes the principal to a compensation claim. This is why registration is a commercial decision, not a formality.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M6 Supplier Relationships','exam','A supplier delivery performance has fallen for three consecutive months. What is the correct first response under a relationship management approach?','Terminate the contract and re-tender the requirement','Withhold all payment until performance recovers','Refer the matter to the legal team for a claim','Hold a structured performance review against the agreed measures and agree a corrective action plan with dates','D','A managed review establishes the cause and puts a dated remedy in place while supply continues. Termination, withholding and litigation are escalation steps that come after a documented remedy has failed.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M8 Procurement and Supply in Practice','exam','Which measure best shows the health of a request for quotation process in a marketplace business?','Quote response rate and time to first quote, measured against the requests issued','The number of suppliers held on the database','Total spend placed in the period','The number of purchase orders raised','A','Response rate and speed measure whether the process actually produces comparable offers, which is the output of the process. Database size, spend and order counts measure scale, not whether the sourcing works.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M5 Commercial Negotiation','exam','A buyer and a long term supplier both want to reduce the cost of delivering the same goods. Which approach fits?','Anchor low on price and concede slowly','Integrative bargaining that expands value by redesigning delivery frequency, packaging and order size','A competitive tender every quarter to test the market','A reverse auction restricted to the incumbent','B','Where both parties share an interest in a lower cost to serve, the gain comes from changing how the work is done rather than from dividing the existing margin. Distributive tactics move value between the parties without creating any.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M4 Ethical and Responsible Sourcing','exam','A buyer discovers that a close friend owns one of the companies bidding for a contract she is evaluating. What must she do?','Declare the conflict of interest and withdraw from the evaluation','Score that bid last to demonstrate impartiality','Say nothing, because the scoring criteria are objective','Ask the friend to withdraw the bid','A','A conflict of interest must be declared and the person removed from the decision, because the risk is to the perceived integrity of the award as much as to the outcome. Self-managed compensation for bias is not a control.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M7 Whole Life Asset Management','hard','Annual demand is 10,000 units, the cost of placing an order is AED 40 and the cost of holding one unit for a year is AED 5. What is the economic order quantity?','200 units','283 units','400 units','800 units','C','The economic order quantity is the square root of two times demand times ordering cost divided by holding cost, which is the square root of 160,000. That gives 400 units per order.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M2 Defining Business Needs','exam','What must a service specification state if performance is to be enforceable?','The internal processes the supplier will follow','The number of staff the supplier will employ','The brand of equipment the supplier will use','Measurable service levels, the method of measurement and the consequence of failure','D','Enforceability depends on a level that can be measured, a method both parties accept, and a stated consequence. Specifying inputs such as staffing or equipment constrains the supplier without guaranteeing the outcome.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M1 Scope and Influence of Procurement and Supply','exam','Which of the following is direct spend?','Contract cleaning of the head office','Fees paid to a marketing agency','Laptops issued to office staff','Raw material that is converted into the product the company sells','D','Direct spend is what goes into the goods or services sold to the customer. Cleaning, agency fees and office equipment support the business but are not part of the product.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M6 Supplier Relationships','exam','Under supplier preferencing, the buyer is a small account but operates in a market the supplier finds attractive. How is the buyer likely to be viewed?','Core','Exploitable','Development','Nuisance','C','Low account value combined with high attractiveness places the buyer in the development box, where the supplier invests to grow the account. Core requires both high value and high attractiveness.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M3 Commercial Contracting','exam','What is an express term of a contract?','A term the parties have specifically agreed and stated','A term read into the contract by statute','A term read in from the custom of the trade','A pre-contractual statement of opinion made during negotiation','A','Express terms are those the parties have actually agreed and set out. Statute and custom produce implied terms, and an opinion expressed in negotiation is generally not a term at all.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M5 Commercial Negotiation','hard','A buyer opens by demanding a price 12 per cent below the market with no supporting analysis. What is the most likely effect?','The supplier concedes quickly to protect the relationship','The anchor lacks credibility, damages trust and can lead the supplier to withdraw or to protect margin by cutting quality','The relationship improves because the buyer has shown resolve','The supplier improves its lead time to compensate','B','An anchor only works while it is credible, and an unsupported demand tells the supplier the buyer has not done the analysis. The supplier response is usually to disengage or to recover the margin elsewhere.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M8 Procurement and Supply in Practice','exam','What is the purpose of an approved supplier list?','To ensure buyers order only from suppliers already assessed for capability, financial standing and compliance','To guarantee that the lowest price is always obtained','To remove the need for written contracts','To replace the specification for repeat purchases','A','The list is a control that moves the assessment work in front of the order rather than after it. It does not set price, replace contracts or define requirements.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M4 Ethical and Responsible Sourcing','exam','Which is the strongest evidence that a supplier meets the labour standards it claims?','A signed self-declaration from the supplier','An independent third party audit report with a corrective action log and a re-audit date','Marketing material describing the supplier values','A long and uneventful trading history with the buyer','B','Independent verification with a tracked corrective action log shows both the finding and what was done about it. Declarations, marketing and trading history are assertions rather than evidence.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M7 Whole Life Asset Management','hard','When is holding buffer stock economically justified?','When both demand and lead time are certain','When the cost of holding stock exceeds the cost of a stockout','When the item sits in the leverage quadrant','When lead time or demand varies and the cost of a stockout exceeds the cost of holding the buffer','D','Buffer stock buys protection against variability, so it is only worth holding when variability exists and the loss it prevents is larger than the cost of holding it. Certainty removes the need for a buffer entirely.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M2 Defining Business Needs','hard','What best describes value analysis applied to an existing bought-in item?','Reducing the margin the supplier earns on the item','Tightening the tolerances stated in the specification','Removing cost that does not contribute to a function the customer values','Increasing order volumes to obtain a lower unit price','C','Value analysis works on the function the item must deliver and strips out cost that buys no function. Margin, tolerance and volume are commercial or technical levers that leave the design untouched.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M5 Commercial Negotiation','exam','A supplier requests a price increase and attributes it to a rise in a published raw material index. What is the strongest buyer response?','Refuse any increase on principle','Accept the increase as the index is objective evidence','Ask for the index, establish the weight of that material in the cost model, and apply the movement only to that share','Re-tender the requirement immediately','C','An index movement affects only the share of the price that the material actually represents, so the increase must be weighted. Blanket refusal and blanket acceptance both ignore the cost structure.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M1 Scope and Influence of Procurement and Supply','exam','What best describes a category management approach?','Organising buying around the department that raises the requisition','Organising buying around each individual supplier','Organising buying around the value of each purchase order','Grouping related spend by supply market and managing it with a strategy built for that market','D','Category management aligns the buying approach to how the supply market behaves rather than to internal structure. That is what allows a single strategy to cover all the spend exposed to the same market forces.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M3 Commercial Contracting','exam','A contract requires the supplier to maintain public liability insurance throughout the term. How should this obligation be treated?','As a contractual obligation of continuing performance which, if breached, gives the buyer remedies including suspension or termination','As unenforceable because insurance is a matter for the supplier','As an implied term that need not be stated','As a warranty given by the buyer to the supplier','A','An insurance covenant is an express, continuing obligation and its breach is a breach of contract like any other. It is not implied, and it is owed by the supplier rather than the buyer.'),
('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L4M6 Supplier Relationships','exam','What should a pre-award supplier appraisal concentrate on?','The lowest bid submitted','Capability, capacity, financial stability, quality systems and compliance evidence','The number of years the company has traded','The number of people the company employs','B','Appraisal tests whether the supplier can actually deliver and survive the contract term. Age and headcount are weak proxies, and price is evaluated separately from capability.')
) as v(certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = 'CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply');

insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select v.* from (values
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M6 Category Management','exam','A newly appointed category manager inherits a category with no strategy and an incumbent supplier of nine years. What comes first?','Analyse the category spend, the supply market and the business requirement before selecting a sourcing approach','Issue a request for quotation to every supplier known to the market','Set a savings target and announce it to the business','Consolidate the volume onto the incumbent to gain scale','A','A category strategy is built from the spend profile, the structure of the supply market and what the business actually needs. Sourcing activity chosen before that analysis is a guess about which lever will work.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M4 Advanced Contract and Financial Management','exam','What does open-book costing actually give the buyer?','Ownership of the supplier intellectual property','A guaranteed reduction in the contract price','Visibility of the supplier cost base, so proposed price changes can be tested against real cost drivers','A right to inspect the supplier tax return','C','Open book converts a price argument into a cost argument by exposing the drivers behind the number. It confers visibility rather than ownership, and it guarantees no particular outcome.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M2 Managing Supply Chain Risk','hard','A critical component is single sourced from one plant in one country. Which mitigation reduces the risk without a large increase in unit cost?','Split the volume equally across two suppliers immediately','Qualify a second source and hold a strategic buffer while leaving the main volume with the incumbent','Extend the contract term with the incumbent supplier','Add a liquidated damages clause covering supply failure','B','Qualification plus a buffer creates a usable alternative without surrendering the volume discount that the incumbent price depends on. Splitting volume immediately raises cost, and a damages clause pays money rather than restoring supply.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M3 Managing Contractual Risk','exam','Why is an unlimited liability clause usually a poor commercial outcome for the buyer as well as the supplier?','It is unlawful in most jurisdictions','It prevents the supplier from renewing its insurance','It binds only the buyer and not the supplier','Suppliers price the unquantified exposure into the bid or decline to bid, so the buyer pays for a risk it may never suffer','D','Risk that cannot be quantified cannot be insured cheaply, so it arrives back in the price or reduces the field. A capped, insurable liability usually buys more real protection per dirham than an uncapped one.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M4 Advanced Contract and Financial Management','exam','Which indicator gives the earliest reliable warning of short term insolvency risk in a supplier?','Gross margin percentage','Return on equity','Asset turnover','The current ratio read together with the trend in cash and in creditor days','D','Short term failure is a liquidity event, so the measures that matter are those describing the ability to pay debts as they fall due. Profitability and efficiency ratios can look healthy right up to a cash failure.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M10 Logistics Management','exam','A buyer consolidates many small inbound orders into one weekly consignment to Jebel Ali. What is the main trade-off?','Lower freight and handling cost per unit, at the price of a longer average order lead time','Lower customs duty, at the price of higher freight','Lower product cost, at the price of lower quality','Lower inventory, at the price of higher freight','A','Consolidation buys scale in transport and handling but forces orders to wait for the departure window. The saving is real and so is the additional stock the longer lead time requires.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M9 Operations Management','hard','A supplier delivering at 92 per cent on time blames the volatile call-off pattern from the buyer. What is the most effective joint action?','Increase the delivery penalty in the contract','Move the volume to an alternative supplier','Share a rolling forecast and agree a frozen period so the supplier can plan capacity and materials','Raise buyer safety stock and accept the performance','C','If the schedule the supplier receives is infeasible, penalties change nothing except the price. A frozen period converts the near horizon into something the supplier can actually resource.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M6 Category Management','exam','Segmentation places a services category in the strategic quadrant. What contract structure fits?','Spot buying against each requirement','A longer term contract with structured governance, a performance regime and a planned exit route','Rolling thirty day terms to preserve flexibility','A single one-off order with an option to repeat','B','Strategic categories justify investment in the relationship, and that investment needs time and governance to pay back. The exit route is what keeps a long contract from becoming a dependency.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M15 Advanced Negotiation','exam','What is the zone of possible agreement in a negotiation?','The price the buyer has been told to achieve','The range between the buyer walk-away point and the supplier walk-away point, within which agreement is possible','The price the supplier opens with','The mid point between the two opening positions','B','The zone is defined by the two reservation points, not by the opening demands or the internal target. If the reservation points do not overlap there is no deal to be made at any level of skill.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M4 Advanced Contract and Financial Management','hard','Supplier A quotes AED 500,000 payable net thirty days. Supplier B quotes AED 498,000 payable in advance. The buyer cost of capital is 12 per cent a year. Which offer is cheaper in present value terms?','Supplier B, by about AED 2,000','They are equivalent once discounted','Supplier A, by about AED 8,000','Supplier A, by about AED 3,000','D','Discounting the A payment by roughly one per cent for the month gives about AED 495,050 today, against AED 498,000 paid immediately to B. The headline saving from B is destroyed by paying thirty days early.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M5 Managing Ethical Procurement and Supply','exam','A small supply market shows suspiciously similar bids year after year. Which control is strongest against bid rigging?','Independent analysis of the cost drivers, together with rotation of the bidder list and monitoring of bid patterns','Asking each bidder to confirm in writing that it did not collude','Always awarding to the lowest bid received','Publishing every price received after the award','A','An independent view of what the work should cost, plus pattern analysis, detects collusion that a signed declaration will never reveal. Publishing prices can actually help a cartel police itself.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M8 Project and Change Management','exam','A project will transfer a service from the incumbent supplier to a new one. What is the principal risk the plan must manage?','The branding of the new supplier','The timing of the launch communication','Continuity of service during cutover, including transfer of data, stock and operational knowledge','The date the contract is signed','C','Transitions fail in the handover of the things that were never written down. The plan must schedule and test the transfer of data, stock and knowledge before the incumbent leaves.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M2 Managing Supply Chain Risk','hard','A risk has a low probability but a catastrophic impact. How should it be treated?','Accepted and monitored, because the expected value is small','Ignored until evidence suggests the probability has risen','Reduced or transferred, with a tested contingency plan, because the impact makes expected value reasoning unsafe','Escalated to the board only once it has occurred','C','Expected value averages away the outcome that would end the business, so it is the wrong tool for catastrophic impact. Such risks are managed by reducing or transferring them and by rehearsing the response.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M7 Achieving Competitive Advantage through the Supply Chain','exam','Which action most directly reduces the bullwhip effect?','Placing larger and less frequent orders','Sharing true end-customer demand data along the chain and reducing order batching','Extending lead times to smooth the schedule','Running more frequent price promotions','B','The bullwhip is amplification of demand signal caused by batching, delay and distorted information. Sharing real demand and cutting batch size attack both causes; promotions and long lead times make it worse.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M3 Managing Contractual Risk','exam','A supplier invokes the force majeure clause because raw material inflation has made the contract unprofitable. How should the buyer assess the claim?','Valid, because inflation is outside the supplier control','Valid if the increase exceeds ten per cent','Valid because the contract does not mention inflation','Not valid, because force majeure covers events that prevent performance, not events that make performance more expensive','D','Force majeure addresses impossibility of performance, not hardship. Price exposure is handled by an indexation or hardship clause, which is a different bargain the parties either made or did not.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M6 Category Management','exam','How should savings from category work be tracked so that they survive audit?','Against an agreed baseline signed off by finance, separating cash releasing reduction from cost avoidance','As the difference between the highest bid received and the awarded bid','As the amount the supplier states it has conceded','As the underspend against the annual budget','A','A savings number is only credible if the baseline and the definition were agreed before the work and can be traced by finance. Bid spreads and supplier claims measure the process, not the effect on the business.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M4 Advanced Contract and Financial Management','exam','A three year fabrication contract is exposed to volatile steel prices. Which pricing mechanism is most appropriate?','A fixed price with a transparent indexation clause tied to a published steel index and a defined material weighting','A firm fixed price for the full three years','Cost plus a percentage of cost','Time and materials with no cap','A','Indexation shares a risk that neither party controls and prices only the element that actually moves. A firm fixed price loads a risk premium into year one, and cost plus percentage rewards the supplier for spending more.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M9 Operations Management','hard','A supplier bottleneck work centre runs at full utilisation with a growing queue in front of it. What does the theory of constraints prescribe?','Raise utilisation of the non-bottleneck work centres to build stock','Exploit and protect the constraint, subordinate the other steps to it, then elevate its capacity','Reduce batch sizes at every work centre simultaneously','Increase finished goods safety stock and leave the process unchanged','B','Output is set by the constraint, so any hour lost there is lost by the whole plant. Improving non-constraints simply builds work in progress in front of the same bottleneck.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M10 Logistics Management','exam','Goods are sold DAP Dubai under Incoterms 2020. Who is responsible for import clearance?','The seller, as part of delivery','The carrier named in the transport document','The buyer','The freight forwarder acting for the seller','C','Under DAP the seller delivers the goods ready for unloading at the named place but import clearance and duty remain with the buyer. Only DDP moves import clearance to the seller.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M5 Managing Ethical Procurement and Supply','exam','A strategic supplier fails an ethical audit on excessive working hours. What is the correct treatment?','Terminate the contract immediately in every case','Take no action while the price remains competitive','Ask the supplier for a written self-declaration of compliance','Agree a time-bound corrective action plan with a re-audit, and escalate to exit only if the plan fails','D','Remediation with a deadline and verification improves conditions for the workers, which termination does not. Exit remains the sanction if the supplier will not or cannot close the finding.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M2 Managing Supply Chain Risk','exam','Which signal of supplier financial distress usually appears earliest to a buyer?','A formal insolvency filing','A report in the trade press','A fall in reported annual profit','Lengthening creditor days and requests to change payment terms or take payment in advance','D','Cash behaviour changes months before published accounts or a filing. A supplier asking to be paid sooner is telling the buyer something the financial statements will confirm much later.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M15 Advanced Negotiation','hard','A supplier makes one large concession early and then refuses to move at all. What is happening and what is the best response?','Good cop bad cop; name the tactic and continue','Salami slicing; ignore it and restate the target','A deliberate anchor and hold; test the concession against the cost model and continue trading variables other than price','Deadline pressure; walk away from the negotiation','C','A single large early move is often designed to define the deal as already generous. Testing it against cost data and opening other variables restores movement without conceding the price frame.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M8 Project and Change Management','exam','Stakeholder analysis places a director as high power and low interest. What engagement approach fits?','Manage closely with detailed weekly working sessions','Keep satisfied with concise, decision-focused updates','Monitor with minimum effort','Keep informed with the full project reporting pack','B','High power and low interest requires enough information to prevent surprise, delivered in a form that respects limited attention. Detailed involvement wastes the influence rather than securing it.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M7 Achieving Competitive Advantage through the Supply Chain','exam','Which supply chain design suits an unpredictable product with a short life cycle?','A responsive chain with capacity buffers and short lead times, accepting a higher unit cost','A lean chain optimised for the lowest possible unit cost','A single low cost offshore source with long lead times','Large batch production to maximise efficiency','A','When demand cannot be forecast, the cost of being wrong exceeds the cost of the capacity that allows a fast response. Lean designs are efficient against stable demand and expensive against volatile demand.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M4 Advanced Contract and Financial Management','exam','How does milestone payment in a services contract protect the buyer?','By reducing the tax due on the contract','By linking cash outflow to verified delivered value, limiting exposure if the supplier fails part way','By accelerating the delivery date','By removing the need for a detailed specification','B','Paying for value that has been delivered and accepted keeps the buyer exposure roughly equal to the work in progress. It does not speed delivery and it does not replace the specification that defines the milestone.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M6 Category Management','hard','Aggregating demand across three business units would cut unit price by eight per cent, but adds three weeks of lead time and creates a single point of failure. How should the decision be made?','On a risk adjusted whole life comparison that includes the extra inventory the longer lead time requires and the cost of a contingency source','On the headline eight per cent price reduction','On the preference of the largest business unit','On the recommendation of the winning supplier','A','The eight per cent is one term in an equation that also contains inventory, service and continuity cost. A decision taken on the price line alone can raise total cost while reporting a saving.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M3 Managing Contractual Risk','exam','How does an indemnity differ from an ordinary claim for damages?','It requires the claimant to prove the loss was foreseeable','It is capped by law at the contract value','It applies only to death and personal injury','It is a promise to reimburse a defined loss without the claimant having to prove breach or remoteness','D','An indemnity creates a debt obligation triggered by the defined event, which avoids the tests of breach, causation and remoteness. That is why suppliers resist broad indemnities far harder than they resist damages clauses.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M9 Operations Management','exam','Four per cent of deliveries are rejected at goods inwards for the same defect. What is the correct long term response?','Inspect one hundred per cent of deliveries at goods inwards','Agree a rebate for each defective unit received','Carry out root cause analysis with the supplier and improve process capability at source','Add a second supplier for the same part','C','Inspection and rebates detect and pay for defects rather than removing them. Capability improvement at the point of manufacture is the only action that changes the defect rate.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M10 Logistics Management','exam','Which storage strategy suits high value, low volume spare parts serving several sites?','Bulk racking of the full range at every site','Consignment stock of the full range at every site','Central stockholding with fast transport, trading inventory cost against distribution cost','No stock at all, ordering only when a failure occurs','C','Centralising pools the demand variability of the sites and cuts the total stock required for the same service level. The premium freight needed to restore responsiveness is normally far cheaper than duplicated stock.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M5 Managing Ethical Procurement and Supply','exam','A supplier in the UAE asks the buyer to fund a small payment to speed up a permit. What must the buyer do?','Pay it and record it as an administrative expense','Pay it because the amount is immaterial','Ask the supplier to make the payment and invoice it as a disbursement','Refuse, record the request, and escalate it, because facilitation payments breach anti-bribery policy and UAE law','D','A facilitation payment is a bribe regardless of size, and routing it through the supplier does not change that. Recording and escalating protects the company and creates the evidence trail.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M2 Managing Supply Chain Risk','hard','How should a business continuity plan covering a critical supplier be validated?','By a simulated disruption exercise with the supplier, measuring actual recovery time against the agreed target','By including a continuity obligation in the contract','By obtaining the supplier written confirmation that a plan exists','By insuring the value of the supply','A','A plan that has never been exercised is a document, not a capability, and the exercise is what reveals the dependencies nobody listed. Contract clauses and insurance allocate the loss but do not restore supply.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M8 Project and Change Management','exam','Buyers who have always sourced by email are resisting a new electronic sourcing tool. What is the most effective change action?','Mandate the tool and monitor compliance centrally','Involve them in the design, show the time saved on their own categories and train them using their real requests for quotation','Withdraw the old process without announcement','Report non-users to their directors','B','Adoption follows demonstrated benefit in the work the person actually does. Mandates and sanctions produce recorded compliance and workaround behaviour.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M4 Advanced Contract and Financial Management','exam','A mid-term price benchmarking exercise should compare the contract against what?','Like for like scope, volume and service level, using current market evidence','The original bid submitted at award','The supplier published list price','The cheapest price available anywhere in any market','A','Benchmarking is only meaningful when the compared offers buy the same thing under the same conditions. Comparing against a different scope produces a number that cannot be acted on.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M6 Category Management','hard','A category has three suppliers, one of which holds ninety per cent of the market. Which lever changes the outcome most durably?','Negotiating harder at each annual review','Committing to a longer contract term in exchange for a discount','Introducing a volume rebate scheme','Developing a credible second supplier so that genuine competitive tension exists at the next award','D','Where one supplier dominates, negotiation, term and rebates all operate inside a structure the buyer has not changed. Creating a real alternative changes the structure and therefore the price.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M15 Advanced Negotiation','exam','Which preparation activity most improves the buyer outcome?','Rehearsing the opening statement','Setting an ambitious opening price','Building a cost model of the supplier price so that each concession can be tested against a real cost driver','Booking a longer meeting than the supplier expects','C','A cost model turns the negotiation from an exchange of positions into an examination of drivers the supplier must answer. Presentation and timing help only once the analysis exists.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M7 Achieving Competitive Advantage through the Supply Chain','exam','What does postponement mean in supply chain design?','Delaying payment to the supplier to improve working capital','Holding generic inventory and delaying final configuration until real demand is known','Delaying the placement of the purchase order until the last moment','Delaying the selection of the supplier until the specification is fixed','B','Postponement holds product in an undifferentiated state so that one pool of stock serves many end variants. It reduces the forecast risk carried by each variant without reducing service.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M3 Managing Contractual Risk','exam','After a data breach by a supplier, the buyer finds liability is capped at the contract value. What contractual position would have protected the buyer best?','Silence on data protection in the contract','A carve-out from the cap for breaches of data protection and confidentiality obligations','A cap set at twelve months of fees rather than contract value','A clause requiring disputes to go to arbitration','B','Caps are appropriate for commercial risk but leave the buyer exposed where the loss can dwarf the contract value. Carving specific categories out of the cap is the standard and negotiable answer.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M9 Operations Management','hard','A supplier process holds 600 units of work in progress and completes 50 units per hour. Using the law relating throughput, work in progress and time, what is the average time a unit spends in the system?','6 hours','8 hours','12 hours','30 hours','C','Average time in the system equals work in progress divided by throughput, that is 600 divided by 50. Cutting work in progress is therefore the direct lever on lead time when throughput is fixed.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M2 Managing Supply Chain Risk','exam','Which is a leading indicator that a supplier is about to miss deliveries?','A missed delivery in the current month','A complaint from the end customer','A falling schedule adherence rate at the supplier together with a rising number of expedite requests','An invoice submitted late','A','Schedule adherence and expediting describe the state of the supplier plan before the customer feels it. Missed deliveries and complaints are the same event observed after the fact.'),
('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply','L5M10 Logistics Management','exam','Under Incoterms 2020, what did the FCA rule add for sellers shipping in containers?','An obligation on the buyer to insure the goods to destination','An obligation on the seller to pay import duty','Transfer of risk to the seller all the way to destination','The buyer may instruct the carrier to issue the seller a transport document stating the goods have been loaded on board','D','The 2020 revision addressed sellers who needed an on-board document for a letter of credit while delivering under FCA. Risk still passes at the named delivery point, not at destination.')
) as v(certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = 'CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply');

insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select v.* from (values
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M2 Global Commercial Strategy','exam','A five forces analysis of a supply market tells the buyer what?','How much profit each supplier earns','The cost base of the leading supplier','Which supplier should be awarded the contract','The structural attractiveness of the market and where buyer power can realistically be exercised','D','Five forces describes the structure that sets the average returns available in the market. It informs where leverage exists; it does not price a bid or select a winner.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M1 Strategic Ethical Leadership','exam','What is the strongest evidence of ethical leadership in a procurement function?','Decisions that hold to the stated policy when doing so costs the organisation money, and that are documented','A published supplier code of conduct','An annual ethics training completion rate','A signed sustainability charter','A','Codes, training and charters state an intention; the decision that costs money and still follows the policy demonstrates it. Documentation is what allows that behaviour to be seen and repeated.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M7 Commercial Data Management','hard','A spend analysis classifies thirty-eight per cent of addressable spend as miscellaneous. What is the priority?','Open negotiations with the largest identified supplier','Improve classification and data quality first, because a strategy built on unclassified spend will target the wrong categories','Set a savings target against the miscellaneous line','Consolidate the miscellaneous spend onto a single supplier','B','Category strategy allocates scarce effort by value and risk, and neither can be seen through an unclassified third of the spend. Acting first on the visible remainder optimises the part that happens to be labelled.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M9 Supply Network Design','exam','A regional network design should be optimised against which objective?','The lowest available warehouse rent','The shortest total distance travelled','Total landed cost and the achieved service level together, including duty, freight, inventory and lead time','The stated preference of the incumbent logistics supplier','C','Network design trades cost against service, and each component moves in a different direction as nodes are added. Optimising a single component reliably produces a network that fails on another.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M3 Global Strategic Supply Chain Operations','exam','A low cost country sourcing programme fails to deliver the savings modelled at award. What is the most common cause?','Poor supplier attitude to the buyer','Costs that were outside the unit price comparison: freight, duty, inventory in transit, quality failure, travel and management time','Unexpected currency gains','Excess capacity in the supply market','B','Low cost country programmes are usually justified on ex works unit price and delivered on total cost. The costs that appear later are structural, not exceptional.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M10 Global Logistics Strategy','hard','A buyer importing into Jebel Ali wants to control the main carriage so it can consolidate volumes from several suppliers. Which Incoterms 2020 rules serve that aim?','DDP, so the seller handles everything to the door','CIF, so the seller arranges carriage and insurance','FOB or FCA, so the buyer nominates the carrier and controls the main carriage','EXW, with the seller completing export clearance','C','Under FOB and FCA the buyer contracts the main carriage and can therefore consolidate across suppliers. DDP and CIF leave carriage with the seller, and under EXW export clearance sits with the buyer, not the seller.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M5 Strategic Programme Leadership','exam','How does a programme differ from a project?','It delivers benefits through a coordinated set of projects and business change activity over time','It simply runs for a longer period','It has a larger budget','It involves more stakeholders','A','A programme exists to realise a benefit that no single project can deliver, and it owns the change as well as the outputs. Duration, budget and stakeholder count are consequences rather than the defining difference.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M4 Future Strategic Challenges for the Profession','exam','What is the most serious strategic consequence of consolidation in a key supply market?','An increase in administrative workload','Higher inbound freight cost','Slower response to requests for quotation','The loss of competitive tension, leaving the buyer as a price taker','D','Consolidation removes the alternatives on which every commercial lever depends. Administration, freight and response times are symptoms that can be managed; the loss of alternatives cannot.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M2 Global Commercial Strategy','exam','On what should a strategic make or buy decision principally turn?','Whether the activity is a core competence and a source of competitive advantage, and only then on relative cost and risk','The current internal cost of performing the activity','The headcount reduction the outsourcing would allow','The lowest quotation received from the market','A','Outsourcing a genuine source of advantage buys a short term cost gain and sells the basis of the business. Cost and risk decide how a non-core activity is delivered, not whether the capability should be kept.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M8 Innovation in Procurement and Supply','exam','What contractual conditions are needed for a buyer to capture supplier innovation?','A tightly fixed specification held constant for the full term','Payment strictly per unit delivered with no other mechanism','Assignment of all supplier background intellectual property to the buyer','An output specification, a gainshare mechanism, and clear rules on the ownership of foreground intellectual property','D','Innovation needs room in the specification, a reason for the supplier to invest, and certainty about who owns what results. Demanding background intellectual property removes the incentive to bring anything.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M7 Commercial Data Management','hard','A procurement dashboard reports AED 4.2m of savings but budgets have not moved. What should the chief procurement officer do?','Publish the savings figure with a note about timing','Raise the savings target for the following year','Reconcile the reported savings to budget and to the profit and loss account with finance, and report cost avoidance separately from cash releasing savings','Reset the baseline so that the figures agree','C','Credibility depends on the reported number being traceable to the accounts, and much genuine value is avoidance that never appears in a budget line. Separating the two categories is honest and defensible; resetting the baseline is not.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M1 Strategic Ethical Leadership','exam','A whistleblowing report alleges that a senior manager steered an award to a favoured bidder. What must the chief procurement officer do?','Ask the manager for an explanation and close the matter if it is satisfactory','Refer the report to the independent investigation route, preserve the evaluation records and remove the manager from the process','Cancel the tender and start again immediately','Wait until the award is complete before acting','B','Independence and evidence preservation are what make the outcome defensible whichever way it falls. Asking the subject of the allegation to self-clear destroys both.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M9 Supply Network Design','exam','Why does risk pooling across a network reduce total safety stock?','Because demand falls when it is aggregated','Because supplier lead times shorten with volume','Because the variability of aggregated demand is proportionally lower than the sum of the individual variabilities','Because suppliers agree to hold the stock instead','C','Independent demand streams do not peak together, so the combined variability grows more slowly than the combined mean. Safety stock follows variability, so pooling lowers it for the same service level.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M3 Global Strategic Supply Chain Operations','hard','A global category strategy must reconcile genuine local requirements with the value of aggregation. Which structure usually works best?','A lead buyer model with a global framework and locally executed call-offs against agreed terms','Fully centralised buying with no local variation permitted','Fully devolved buying with a shared supplier list','Global supplier selection with local price negotiation against each order','A','A lead buyer model captures the scale in the terms while leaving the operational detail where the knowledge sits. Full centralisation breaks on local requirements, and full devolution surrenders the aggregation.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M10 Global Logistics Strategy','exam','An AED based buyer signs a three year contract priced in euro. How should the currency exposure be managed?','Ignored, because the dirham is pegged to the euro','Passed entirely to the supplier by contract','Removed by paying the whole contract in advance','Hedged with forward cover for the contracted exposure, or removed by contracting in dirhams and accepting the risk premium in the price','D','The dirham is pegged to the US dollar, not the euro, so a euro contract carries real exposure. Hedging or contracting in dirhams prices the risk deliberately rather than leaving it open.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M5 Strategic Programme Leadership','exam','Who should own the benefits realisation plan for a procurement transformation programme?','The programme manager who runs the delivery','The business owner who will operate the changed process and be measured on the benefit','The procurement function that identified the opportunity','The finance function that will book the saving','B','Benefits are realised in operations after the programme closes, so ownership must sit with the person who will still be there and accountable. Delivery, sourcing and accounting roles support that owner.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M4 Future Strategic Challenges for the Profession','exam','Which capability will most shape the procurement function of a marketplace business over the coming decade?','More frequent supplier visits','A larger buying team','Longer contract terms','Data and analytical capability, including automated sourcing and supplier intelligence','D','The differentiating work is moving to the design of rules, models and data that operate at a scale no team can match manually. Headcount and term length do not change what the function can see or decide.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M2 Global Commercial Strategy','hard','A buyer represents three per cent of the revenue of every supplier in a concentrated market. What is a realistic strategy?','Demand open book costing and a ten per cent reduction','Aggregate demand with other buyers, redesign the requirement to widen the eligible market, or accept the market price and compete on other levers','Threaten to exit the market entirely','Move to spot buying to keep options open','B','A small account cannot manufacture leverage by assertion, so the options are to become a larger account, to change what is being bought, or to stop paying for leverage that does not exist. Threats from a weak position damage the supply position further.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M8 Innovation in Procurement and Supply','exam','Why does early supplier involvement deliver most of its value at the design stage?','Because most of the eventual cost is committed by design decisions while it is still cheap to change them','Because it shortens the tender period','Because it removes the need for a formal contract','Because it lowers inbound freight cost','A','A large share of whole life cost is locked in by design long before the first order is placed. Involvement after the design is frozen can only optimise what has already been committed.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M1 Strategic Ethical Leadership','exam','When are supply chain sustainability targets credible?','When they appear in the annual report','When they are approved by the board','When they are measured with supplier level data, independently verified, and tied to award criteria and contract terms','When they are agreed with an industry trade body','C','A target changes behaviour only when it is measured at the level where the emissions or conditions occur and carries a commercial consequence. Publication and approval record the ambition, not the effect.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M9 Supply Network Design','exam','Dual sourcing on a seventy thirty split costs more per unit than a single source. What does the premium buy?','A qualified alternative kept live, preserving continuity and competitive tension at the next award','A reduced specification burden on the buying team','Elimination of the need for safety stock','Improved payment terms from both suppliers','A','The minority share is what keeps the second supplier tooled, qualified and interested. That capability is the thing being purchased, and it disappears the moment the volume goes to zero.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M7 Commercial Data Management','hard','Which control most improves the quality of quotation comparison in a request for quotation marketplace?','Inviting more suppliers to each event','Allowing longer quotation windows','A structured, mandatory quotation schema so that line items, currency, Incoterm and lead time are captured comparably','A free text quotation form that lets suppliers explain their offer','C','Comparison is only possible when the offers are expressed on the same axes, and free text guarantees they are not. More suppliers and more time increase the volume of incomparable data.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M3 Global Strategic Supply Chain Operations','exam','What is the principal strategic reason for nearshoring part of a supply base?','Lower labour cost than the current source','Shorter and more resilient lead times, and lower volatility in total landed cost','Easier communication in a shared language','Lower customs duty in every case','B','Nearshoring buys responsiveness and reduces exposure to long, fragile transport chains. Labour is usually dearer and duty depends entirely on the trade agreements that apply.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M5 Strategic Programme Leadership','exam','A programme is late and over budget. What must the governance board require first?','A replacement supplier for the delayed workstream','Additional resource to recover the schedule','An increase in scope to justify the spend','A re-baselined plan with the business case re-tested for continued viability','D','The first governance question is whether the programme is still worth completing on the facts as they now stand. Resourcing and scope decisions taken before that test can add cost to an unviable case.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M2 Global Commercial Strategy','exam','What is the principal risk of a supplier rationalisation programme?','A heavier administrative workload','A weaker technical specification','Greater dependency and reduced competition if rationalisation passes the point where alternatives remain viable','Longer inbound lead times only','C','Rationalisation delivers scale by removing alternatives, which is exactly what creates dependency past a certain point. The optimum is where scale is captured and a credible alternative still exists.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M4 Future Strategic Challenges for the Profession','hard','How does circular economy thinking most change procurement decisions?','It requires recycled content in packaging','It adds a sustainability clause to contracts','It adds a reporting line to the annual review','It moves the buying decision to whole life and end of life value, including take-back, reuse and residual value','D','A circular model changes what is being bought and how value is recovered at the end, which alters the economics of the decision itself. Clauses, packaging rules and reporting sit inside the old decision frame.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M10 Global Logistics Strategy','exam','What does holding regional stock in the Jebel Ali Free Zone allow a distributor to do?','Avoid customs formalities permanently','Hold imported stock without paying import duty until the goods enter the UAE mainland market','Sell into the mainland market free of duty','Disregard rules of origin when re-exporting','B','Free zone treatment defers the duty point until the goods are imported into the mainland, which helps a business re-exporting across the region. Goods entering the mainland are cleared and dutied normally.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M8 Innovation in Procurement and Supply','exam','What is the most common barrier to supplier led innovation?','Buyer commercial behaviour that offers the supplier no route to propose an idea and no reward for doing so','A lack of technical capability among suppliers','The immaturity of the available technology','Contract terms that are too short','A','Suppliers withhold ideas when the predictable outcome is that the saving is taken and the intellectual property claimed. Creating a route and a share is what changes the behaviour.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M1 Strategic Ethical Leadership','exam','A chief procurement officer discovers that a long standing supplier is owned by a relative of a board member. What is the correct response?','Terminate the supplier to remove any doubt','Disclose to the audit committee, review the award history for value for money, and route future awards through an independent evaluation','Continue as before, since the prices paid have been competitive','Ask the board member to resign from the board','B','The issue is the integrity of past and future decisions, which is settled by disclosure, review and independent evaluation. Termination punishes a supplier that may have done nothing wrong and destroys the evidence trail.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M9 Supply Network Design','hard','Adding a second distribution node shortens delivery lead time but adds fixed cost and duplicated stock. How should the decision be modelled?','As a trade-off between the service level achieved and total network cost, tested against realistic demand scenarios','As a straight comparison of the fixed costs of the two options','As a customer preference survey','As a decision for the incumbent logistics supplier to recommend','A','The value of the second node is the service it buys, and its cost includes the inventory it duplicates as well as the facility. Scenario testing shows whether the answer survives demand that differs from the plan.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M7 Commercial Data Management','exam','Why does supplier master data governance matter commercially?','It reduces the cost of data storage','It speeds up email delivery to suppliers','It satisfies the external auditor','Duplicate and stale supplier records corrupt spend analysis, weaken payment control and hide duplicate or fraudulent payees','D','Every commercial conclusion drawn from spend data inherits the quality of the supplier master. The same weakness is the standard route for duplicate payment and payee fraud.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M3 Global Strategic Supply Chain Operations','exam','What should a global framework agreement fix centrally?','Local delivery dates for each site','The named local contacts at each site','Commercial terms, the price mechanism, liability and governance, leaving volumes and call-off detail to local execution','Every term, so that no local variation is possible','C','The framework captures the terms that only scale can win, and leaves operational detail where it can be managed. Fixing everything centrally makes the agreement unusable at the sites it was written for.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M5 Strategic Programme Leadership','exam','What is the strongest control over a large procurement transformation programme?','Weekly status meetings with all workstream leads','A detailed critical path schedule','A large programme management office','Stage gates with explicit go or no-go criteria owned by an accountable sponsor','D','A stage gate forces a decision to continue against defined evidence, which is the only control that can stop a failing programme. Meetings, schedules and offices report progress rather than authorise it.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M4 Future Strategic Challenges for the Profession','hard','Automating quotation evaluation raises which governance risk first?','Awards will take longer to complete','Software licence costs will rise','Bias and opacity in the scoring rules, so that award decisions cannot be explained or defended when challenged','Fewer suppliers will choose to participate','C','An automated award still has to be justified to an unsuccessful bidder and to an auditor. If the rule cannot be explained, the decision cannot be defended regardless of how good the outcome is.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M2 Global Commercial Strategy','exam','A buyer wants to move a category away from price-only competition. What must change first?','The award criteria and their weightings, so that quality, lead time and whole life cost carry defined weight','The list of suppliers invited to bid','The payment terms offered to suppliers','The length of the contract term','A','Bidders optimise against the criteria they are scored on, so behaviour changes only when the scoring changes. Supplier lists and terms do not alter what the competition rewards.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M8 Innovation in Procurement and Supply','exam','Why do gainshare mechanisms most often fail?','Suppliers refuse to participate in them','The baseline and the measurement rules were not defined precisely before the work started','They are unlawful in most contracts','They require full open book costing to operate','B','Gainshare turns on the difference between an agreed baseline and a measured outcome, so an imprecise baseline guarantees a dispute. The mechanism itself is sound when the arithmetic is agreed in advance.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M9 Supply Network Design','exam','How should the resilience of a supply network be tested?','By counting the number of suppliers in the network','By reviewing the contractual continuity obligations','By modelling the loss of the largest single node and measuring the time and cost required to restore service','By increasing inventory at every node','C','Resilience is a measured recovery time against a specific failure, not a count of suppliers or clauses. Modelling the loss reveals the dependencies that the organisation chart hides.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M1 Strategic Ethical Leadership','hard','When does a supplier code of conduct become meaningful rather than decorative?','When it is signed by every supplier at onboarding','When compliance is verified in proportion to risk, non-compliance carries consequences, and the buyer own payment and forecasting behaviour supports the standards it demands','When it is published on the corporate website','When it restates the applicable law in full','B','A code works when it is verified, enforced, and not contradicted by the way the buyer actually behaves. Late payment and volatile forecasts push suppliers into the very practices the code prohibits.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M3 Global Strategic Supply Chain Operations','exam','What is the strategic case for investing in a supplier relationship management programme?','It produces better structured supplier meetings','It improves supplier satisfaction survey scores','It reduces the number of contractual disputes','It unlocks value that only the relationship can deliver: innovation, priority during scarcity and cost reduction beyond price','D','Governance, satisfaction and fewer disputes are by-products; the investment case rests on value that a transactional relationship cannot produce. That value has to be measured for the programme to survive scrutiny.'),
('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)','L6M2 Global Commercial Strategy','exam','A board asks why procurement should be involved in a proposed acquisition of a competitor. What is the strongest answer?','Overlapping supply bases, contract change of control clauses and combined volumes materially affect both the valuation and the integration plan','Procurement can negotiate the professional fees for the transaction','Procurement holds the supplier contact details the integration team will need','Procurement can arrange the logistics of combining the two warehouses','A','Supply contracts carry change of control, termination and pricing consequences that show up directly in the deal value and in the first year of integration. Fees, contacts and logistics are administrative contributions rather than strategic ones.')
) as v(certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = 'MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)');

insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select v.* from (values
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 1: Supply Management Core','exam','A new component is required and there is no incumbent supplier. What is the correct first step?','Issue a request for quotation to the three largest firms in the industry','Negotiate with the vendor the design engineer prefers','Analyse the supply market and issue a request for information to establish capability before seeking prices','Place a trial order with the fastest respondent','C','Prices cannot be evaluated until the buyer knows who can actually make the part and to what capability. A request for information establishes the field, and the priced competition follows.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 1: Supply Management Core','exam','Total cost of ownership covers acquisition costs, ownership costs and what else?','Post-ownership costs such as disposal, decommissioning and residual value','Freight and duty only','Purchase price and taxes only','Scheduled maintenance only','A','Total cost of ownership follows the item from the decision to buy through to its removal from the business. Excluding the end of life stage understates the cost of assets that are expensive to dispose of.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 2: Supply Management Integration','hard','A supplier reports a process capability index of 0.9 on a critical dimension. How should the buyer read this?','The process is capable and needs no action','The process is centred on the target','The measurement system must be faulty','The process is not capable, the expected defect rate is material, and improvement is required before volume release','D','A capability index below one means the process spread exceeds the tolerance, so defects are expected as a normal outcome. Releasing volume against that index transfers the cost of sorting to the buyer.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 1: Supply Management Core','exam','When is a contractual right to audit supplier records most valuable?','When the price is firm fixed for the full term','When the pricing is cost based, incentive based or operated on an open book','When the supplier is a small business','When the contract runs for less than one year','B','Audit rights matter where the price the buyer pays is derived from supplier cost or performance data. Under a firm fixed price the supplier cost base does not change what is owed.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 3: Leadership and Transformation in Supply Management','exam','A supply organisation buys reactively with no category strategies. What should its leader do first?','Segment the spend and build category strategies for the highest value and highest risk categories','Recruit additional buyers to cover the workload','Purchase an electronic sourcing platform','Announce an annual savings target','A','Segmentation shows where the value and the risk actually sit, which is what tells the leader where to spend scarce capability. Headcount, tools and targets applied without that view are spread evenly across unequal categories.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 2: Supply Management Integration','exam','What best integrates supply management with the finance function?','Holding a monthly meeting between the two teams','A savings methodology agreed with finance and reconciled to budget, so reported value can be traced into the accounts','Sharing the same software platform','Placing both functions under one reporting line','B','Integration is proven when the two functions agree on what a saving is and can find it in the same numbers. Meetings, tools and structure do not settle the definition that causes the disagreement.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 1: Supply Management Core','hard','A supplier will only sign a three year agreement if prices rise annually with the consumer price index. What is the strongest counter proposal?','Accept the index as it is an objective published measure','Refuse all increases for the full term','Offer a two year term instead of three','Index only the cost elements that genuinely move, weighted by their share of the cost model, with a cap and a reopener','D','A general inflation index is not a measure of the cost of this product, and applying it to the whole price indexes profit as well as cost. Weighted indexation with a cap prices the real exposure and bounds it.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 2: Supply Management Integration','exam','What is the purpose of supplier segmentation?','To reduce the total number of suppliers','To rank suppliers from cheapest to dearest','To allocate management effort and choose a relationship type according to value and risk','To decide payment terms for each supplier','C','Segmentation exists because management attention is scarce and suppliers are not equally important. Rationalisation and terms may follow from the segmentation but are not its purpose.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 1: Supply Management Core','exam','At what point does a purchase order become a binding contract?','When it is issued by the buyer','When it is received by the supplier','When it is filed in the buyer system','When it is accepted by the supplier, either expressly or by beginning performance','D','A purchase order is an offer until the supplier accepts it. Acceptance can be express or shown by conduct such as shipping the goods.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 3: Leadership and Transformation in Supply Management','exam','What is the most defensible way to report supply management performance to a chief executive?','The number of contracts signed in the period','The reduction in the number of active suppliers','Cost, risk and service outcomes reconciled to the financial statements and to operational service measures','The average purchase order cycle time','C','A chief executive needs the effect on the business, expressed in the same numbers the business reports. Activity measures describe the function rather than its contribution.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 2: Supply Management Integration','hard','Annual demand for a part is 1,200 units, lead time is four weeks, weekly demand has a standard deviation of five units, and the target service level requires a factor of 1.65. What safety stock is required?','About 17 units','About 8 units','About 33 units','About 50 units','A','Demand variability over the lead time is five multiplied by the square root of four, which is ten units. Multiplying by the service factor of 1.65 gives roughly seventeen units.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 1: Supply Management Core','exam','Which sourcing method fits a well defined, repeat commodity with many capable suppliers?','Sole source negotiation with the incumbent','Competitive bidding through a request for quotation with clearly comparable line items','A joint design partnership','Consortium buying through a third party only','B','Where the requirement is settled and alternatives exist, competition is the cheapest and fastest way to establish the market price. Negotiation and partnership are for situations where competition is not available or not sufficient.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 2: Supply Management Integration','exam','How should a supplier scorecard be constructed?','Weighted entirely to price performance','From data the buyer already captures, using definitions agreed with the supplier, and shared with the supplier regularly','Kept confidential from the supplier to preserve leverage','Produced once a year at the contract review','B','A scorecard changes behaviour only if the supplier sees it, accepts the definitions and can act on it between reviews. Data the buyer must create by hand stops being collected within two quarters.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 3: Leadership and Transformation in Supply Management','hard','A chief procurement officer is told to cut headcount by twenty per cent while increasing the share of spend under management. What is the defensible plan?','Stop managing the tail suppliers','Reduce the frequency of contract reviews','Reduce the strategic category team and keep transactional coverage','Automate low value transactional buying to release capacity into category work, and evidence it with cycle time and coverage data','D','The only way to do more with less is to remove work rather than to remove attention from valuable categories. Automation of the transactional layer is where the volume of work sits, and the data proves the shift happened.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 1: Supply Management Core','exam','Which is a legitimate basis for a sole source award?','The buyer has an established relationship with the salesperson','The supplier has offered the lowest price','A genuine technical or intellectual property constraint, documented and approved under the delegation policy','The internal customer has asked for that supplier by name','C','Sole sourcing is defensible when competition is genuinely unavailable and the reason is recorded and approved. Preference, price and familiarity are not constraints on the market.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 2: Supply Management Integration','exam','What does a consignment inventory arrangement change?','Ownership and the working capital burden stay with the supplier until the buyer consumes the stock','Liability for quality defects moves to the buyer on delivery','Freight cost moves from the supplier to the buyer','Demand risk moves to the end customer','A','Consignment separates physical possession from ownership, so the buyer holds the stock but does not fund it. Quality, freight and demand obligations are set by the rest of the contract.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 1: Supply Management Core','exam','Under the UAE Civil Transactions Law, what is the effect of a reply that purports to accept an offer but changes its terms?','It is a valid acceptance and the contract forms on the new terms','It forms a binding contract on the original terms','It operates as a rejection of the offer and constitutes a new offer','It is void and no further negotiation is possible','C','A qualified acceptance does not match the offer, so it destroys the original offer and puts a fresh one on the table. The original offeror is then free to accept, reject or counter.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 2: Supply Management Integration','exam','What is the strongest control against maverick spending?','Circulating a policy reminder to budget holders','Making the compliant route the easiest one, through catalogues, call-off contracts and a workflow that blocks off-contract orders','Reporting breaches to management each month','Delivering procurement training to budget holders','B','People take the route that is fastest to their outcome, so compliance has to be built into the path rather than requested. Reporting and training address a behaviour whose cause is process friction.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 3: Leadership and Transformation in Supply Management','hard','A transformation programme has stalled because category managers are absorbed in daily firefighting. What is the leadership fix?','Protect their capacity by moving transactional work out of the team and setting explicit category deliverables with dates','Extend the programme deadline to reduce pressure','Bring in consultants to run the category work','Change the reporting structure of the team','A','Firefighting wins against strategy every time unless the operational load is physically removed and the strategic work is scheduled. Deadlines, consultants and structures leave the same people with the same in-tray.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 1: Supply Management Core','exam','What is a request for information intended to achieve?','A comparable set of prices','A contract award','Execution of a non-disclosure agreement','An understanding of market capability, capacity and interest before a priced competition is run','D','A request for information shapes the competition by establishing who can do the work. Asking for prices before that is understood produces numbers that cannot be compared.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 2: Supply Management Integration','exam','What is the principal risk of unilaterally extending supplier payment terms to one hundred and twenty days?','An increase in the tax payable on purchases','Slower physical delivery of goods','Suppliers will increase order minimums','Suppliers finance the working capital at a higher cost than the buyer and price it back, or fail, raising total cost and supply risk','D','Extended terms move cash rather than remove cost, and the supplier funding rate is usually higher than the buyer rate. The cost returns in price, in service or in a failure.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 1: Supply Management Core','exam','When should a confidentiality agreement be executed in a sourcing process?','Before commercially sensitive information is exchanged with bidders','At the point of contract award','After the first delivery has been accepted','Only for suppliers classified as strategic','A','The protection must exist before the information moves, because disclosure cannot be undone afterwards. Applying it only to strategic suppliers leaves the specification exposed during the competition.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 3: Leadership and Transformation in Supply Management','hard','How should a local content or supplier diversity programme in the UAE be measured?','By the number of local suppliers registered on the database','By qualified local suppliers actually winning and delivering work, tracked as a share of addressable spend','By the publication of a local content policy','By attendance at supplier engagement events','B','Registration and events measure effort; awarded and delivered spend measures whether the market access is real. Tracking against addressable spend prevents the number being flattered by categories no local supplier could serve.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 2: Supply Management Integration','exam','Why do cross functional sourcing teams produce better outcomes?','They spread the administrative workload','They shorten the approval chain','They bring requirement, technical and commercial knowledge into the specification and into the award criteria','They reduce the number of meetings required','C','The quality of an award is set by the specification and the criteria, and those need knowledge no single function holds. The team exists to improve the decision, not to share the work.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 1: Supply Management Core','exam','Which contract type transfers the most cost risk to the supplier?','Firm fixed price','Fixed price with economic price adjustment','Cost plus incentive fee','Time and materials with a not to exceed ceiling','A','Under a firm fixed price the supplier absorbs every cost movement, favourable or adverse. Each of the other types returns some portion of that risk to the buyer.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 2: Supply Management Integration','exam','A buyer wants supplier managed replenishment for low value, high frequency items. Which mechanism fits?','Spot buying each time stock runs low','A blanket order with manual call-off by the storekeeper','Vendor managed inventory with agreed minimum and maximum levels and shared consumption data','A weekly request for quotation to three suppliers','C','Vendor managed inventory removes the buyer transaction entirely and lets the supplier plan against real consumption. The other options keep a manual step in a category where the transaction cost dominates.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 3: Leadership and Transformation in Supply Management','exam','A chief procurement officer learns that a category manager accepted an expensive gift shortly after making an award. What is the correct response?','Ask for the gift to be returned and take the matter no further','Investigate under the policy, review the award for integrity, and apply the disciplinary process','Record the gift in the register retrospectively','Move the manager to a different category','B','Both the conduct and the decision it may have influenced have to be examined, and the process must be the formal one. Quiet corrections leave the award unexamined and the precedent set.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 1: Supply Management Core','hard','Three suppliers quote for 10,000 units. Supplier A quotes AED 12.00 with a 3 per cent defect rate, supplier B quotes AED 11.40 with 7 per cent, and supplier C quotes AED 12.20 with 1 per cent. Each defective unit costs AED 20 to replace and rework. Which offer is cheapest overall?','Supplier B, because it has the lowest unit price','Supplier A, at about AED 128,000','All three are within rounding of each other','Supplier C, at about AED 124,000','D','Adding the defect cost gives effective unit costs of about 12.60 for A, 12.80 for B and 12.40 for C, so C totals roughly AED 124,000 against 126,000 and 128,000. The lowest quoted price is the most expensive once quality cost is counted.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 2: Supply Management Integration','exam','What best measures the efficiency of the sourcing cycle?','The number of requests for quotation issued','Elapsed time from approved requisition to awarded contract, reported by category','The number of suppliers contacted per event','Savings delivered per buyer','B','Cycle time from requisition to award measures what the internal customer experiences end to end. Counts of activity can rise while the business waits just as long.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 3: Leadership and Transformation in Supply Management','exam','What is the principal risk of a supply management function that reports into finance?','It is measured only on cost, so risk, innovation and service value carry no weight in its objectives','It will lose access to budget','Supplier payments will slow down','Contracts will be drafted less carefully','A','Reporting lines shape the measures, and a cost-only measure makes cost-only decisions rational for the function. The other effects are possible but are not caused by the reporting line.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 1: Supply Management Core','exam','How should liquidated damages be set in a supply contract?','At the highest figure the supplier will accept','At the buyer full loss including consequential loss','As a realistic pre-estimate of the loss per day or per unit of delay, subject to a cap','At one hundred per cent of the contract value','C','A pre-estimate with a cap is enforceable and can be priced by the supplier. A figure set to punish invites challenge and a figure set at full loss is priced back into the bid.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 2: Supply Management Integration','hard','Planned orders released by the material requirements plan are expedited almost every time. What is the most likely root cause?','The supplier is failing to perform against its commitments','Inbound freight is unreliable','Safety stock levels are set too low','The planning lead time parameters understate the real lead time, so the plan is infeasible on the day it is released','D','If the planned lead time is shorter than reality, every order is late before it is placed and expediting becomes the normal process. Adjusting stock or blaming the supplier leaves the parameter that creates the shortfall untouched.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 1: Supply Management Core','exam','A new entrant bids well below its own likely cost in order to win entry to the account. What is the correct response?','Accept it, because the buyer duty is to take the lowest price','Report the bid to the competition authority','Test the bid for price realism and sustainability, because an unsustainable price usually returns as claims, quality failure or exit','Ask all bidders to submit revised prices','C','An unsustainable price is a risk transfer disguised as a saving, and the buyer pays it later. Testing realism is a normal part of evaluation and can be documented in the award.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 3: Leadership and Transformation in Supply Management','exam','What most clearly shows that supply management has strategic influence in a business?','A prominent position on the corporate intranet','The size of the buying team','The volume of purchase orders processed','Involvement before the requirement is fixed, while specification and make or buy decisions are still open','D','Influence is the ability to shape the decision, and most of the cost is committed before a requisition exists. Being consulted after the specification is set limits the function to negotiating a settled outcome.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 2: Supply Management Integration','exam','A quality defect from a supplier has reached the end customer. What is the immediate supply action?','Containment: identify and quarantine affected stock at every point in the chain, then pursue root cause with the supplier','Move the volume to an alternative supplier','Submit a claim for damages under the contract','Introduce permanent one hundred per cent inspection','A','Containment stops further defective product reaching customers while the cause is still unknown. Resourcing, claims and inspection all come after the bleeding has been stopped.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 1: Supply Management Core','hard','A supplier requests a six per cent price increase citing energy costs. Energy is fifteen per cent of its cost base and the relevant energy index has risen by twenty per cent. What increase is justified on that evidence?','Six per cent as requested','About three per cent','About twenty per cent','No increase at all','B','Twenty per cent applied to the fifteen per cent of cost that energy represents gives about three per cent of the total price. The request is double the movement the evidence supports.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 3: Leadership and Transformation in Supply Management','exam','When is a supply risk register actually useful?','When it lists every conceivable risk to the supply base','When it is reviewed once a year at the management meeting','When it is owned exclusively by the procurement function','When each entry has a named owner, a dated mitigation and a trigger that forces action','D','A register drives behaviour only when someone is accountable and something makes the action happen at the right moment. Completeness and periodic review produce a document rather than a control.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 2: Supply Management Integration','exam','Which contract mechanism best aligns a logistics provider to service rather than to volume?','Payment per kilometre driven','A service level regime with deductions and earn-back tied to on-time in-full performance','Cost plus a fixed percentage','An annual lump sum fee','B','Paying against the outcome the buyer values makes service the provider commercial priority. Distance, cost plus and lump sum arrangements pay for activity or presence regardless of performance.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 1: Supply Management Core','exam','What is the principal benefit of standardising specifications across multiple sites?','Documentation becomes easier to maintain','Internal approvals move faster','Volume is aggregated onto fewer part numbers, lowering price, inventory and obsolescence risk','Fewer suppliers need to be audited each year','C','Standardisation is a demand-side lever that reduces the number of distinct items the business must buy and hold. Administrative gains are real but small beside the inventory and price effect.'),
('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)','Exam 3: Leadership and Transformation in Supply Management','hard','A chief procurement officer must justify continued investment in a supplier relationship management programme. What is the strongest evidence?','Value measured from the managed suppliers compared against a matched set of unmanaged suppliers','Results of the annual supplier satisfaction survey','The number of governance meetings held during the year','The number of suppliers enrolled in the programme','A','A matched comparison isolates the effect of the programme from what would have happened anyway. Surveys, meetings and enrolment counts measure the activity that the investment funds, not its return.')
) as v(certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = 'CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)');

insert into public.certification_exam_question
  (certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
select v.* from (values
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Design','exam','What are the level one processes of the SCOR reference model?','Source, Store, Ship, Sell and Return','Plan, Source, Make, Deliver, Return and Enable','Plan, Buy, Build and Bill','Design, Build and Run','B','SCOR describes a supply chain through those six top level processes, which is what allows two organisations to compare their operations. The other lists are invented groupings that no reference model uses.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Planning and Execution','exam','What does the sales and operations planning process reconcile?','Purchase orders against supplier invoices','The demand forecast against the marketing campaign plan','Demand and supply plans against the financial plan, at an aggregate level over a rolling horizon','The production schedule against the shipping schedule','C','Sales and operations planning is the monthly executive process that brings demand, supply and money into one agreed plan. Detailed order and schedule reconciliation happens at the execution level below it.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Design','hard','A product has highly variable demand and a long supplier lead time. Which design lever reduces inventory most effectively?','Placing larger and less frequent orders','Negotiating a lower unit price','Adding more suppliers of the same item','Shortening or decoupling the lead time, for example through postponement or a regional decoupling point','D','Safety stock is driven by demand variability across the lead time, so shortening the lead time attacks the larger of the two terms. Price and supplier count do not change the exposure the stock is covering.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Improvement and Best Practices','exam','What does the cash to cash cycle time measure?','How long cash is tied up between paying suppliers and collecting from customers','The gross margin earned on each order','The accuracy of inventory records','The proportion of orders shipped complete','A','The measure combines inventory days and receivable days less payable days, so it shows the working capital the chain consumes. It is the metric that links supply chain design directly to cash.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Planning and Execution','exam','What does available to promise represent?','All inventory currently on hand','The forecast demand for the period','The uncommitted portion of on-hand inventory and planned production that can still be promised to customers','The master production schedule for the period','C','Available to promise is what remains after existing customer commitments are deducted from supply. Promising against total stock rather than the uncommitted balance is how double promising happens.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Improvement and Best Practices','exam','Which lean concept most directly shortens the lead time of a quotation process?','Removing waiting time and handoffs from the value stream between request and quotation','Adding a review step to catch errors before issue','Processing quotations in larger daily batches','Adding staff to absorb the peak workload','A','In an information process almost all the elapsed time is queueing between steps rather than working time. Reviews and batching add queue, and extra staff raise cost without changing the flow.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Design','hard','A company serving the GCC from a Jebel Ali hub must choose between one regional distribution centre and three national ones. What analysis settles it?','A comparison of warehouse rent per square metre','A comparison of total cost to serve against the service level each design achieves, including inventory, transport, duty and customs handling','A comparison of the headcount each design requires','The stated preference of the largest customer','B','Adding nodes buys service and costs inventory, facilities and handling, so only a total cost to serve comparison shows the net effect. Rent and headcount are components of that total, not substitutes for it.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Planning and Execution','exam','Forecast error for one product family has risen sharply. What should be checked first?','Whether a different forecasting model would fit the history better','Whether safety stock should be increased to cover the error','Whether the sales team should be asked to explain the variance','Whether a real change in demand occurred, such as a promotion, a new customer or a data error, before the model is changed','D','Models should only be changed once the data has been shown to be sound and the demand change understood. Refitting a model to contaminated history embeds the error in every future period.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Improvement and Best Practices','exam','What must the measure phase of a DMAIC project produce?','A validated measurement system and baseline data on the defect or cycle time being improved','A proposed solution ready for approval','A control chart of the improved process','A signed project charter','A','Measure establishes that the data can be trusted and records where the process started, which is what later proves the improvement. Charter, solution and control belong to define, improve and control respectively.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Design','exam','When is upstream vertical integration most attractive?','When the supply market contains many capable competing suppliers','When input prices are falling steadily','When the input is a low value consumable','When the input is critical, the supply market is concentrated, and the firm can operate the activity at competitive scale','D','Integration buys control at the cost of capital and flexibility, so it pays only where the market cannot be relied on and scale can be reached. In a competitive market the same supply can be bought more cheaply than it can be made.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Planning and Execution','hard','Cost of goods sold is AED 24 million a year and inventory turns six times. What is the average inventory value?','AED 2 million','AED 4 million','AED 6 million','AED 12 million','B','Average inventory equals cost of goods sold divided by the number of turns, that is 24 million divided by six. Each additional turn releases cash equal to the difference between successive results.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Improvement and Best Practices','exam','What is the correct sequence for a supplier corrective action process?','Root cause, containment, verification, prevention','Containment, verification, prevention, root cause','Containment, root cause analysis, corrective action, verification of effectiveness','Root cause, prevention, containment, closure','C','Containment protects the customer first, root cause explains the failure, corrective action removes it and verification proves the action worked. Closing without verification is the most common reason the same defect returns.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Design','exam','Which product profile calls for a responsive rather than an efficient supply chain?','A functional product with stable, predictable demand','A commodity with a long and stable life cycle','A low margin product sold on price','An innovative product with a short life cycle, volatile demand and a high cost of stockout','D','Responsiveness is bought with capacity and shorter lead times, and it pays where the cost of missing demand is high. For stable, low margin products the same investment simply raises cost.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Planning and Execution','exam','What does distribution requirements planning do?','Explodes bills of material into component requirements','Converts time-phased distribution centre requirements into a consolidated central supply plan','Converts customer orders into invoices','Converts capacity requirements into labour hours','B','Distribution requirements planning works backwards from demand at each stocking location to what the central source must supply and when. Material requirements planning performs the equivalent role inside manufacturing.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Improvement and Best Practices','hard','A supplier reports 88 per cent on-time delivery while the buyer receiving dock records 74 per cent for the same shipments. What is the most likely explanation?','The two measures use different clocks and windows, typically ship date against receipt date, so transit time is not counted in the supplier figure','The supplier is deliberately falsifying its performance data','The receiving team is slow to book goods into the system','Demand rose during the period and distorted the comparison','A','Performance disputes of this shape are nearly always definitional rather than dishonest. Agreeing one measurement point and one time window resolves the gap before any performance discussion is possible.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Design','exam','A buyer uses one tier one supplier which in turn buys from several tier two suppliers, all located in the same region. What risk does this create?','None, because tier one has diversified its own supply','Price risk only, since regional costs move together','A hidden concentration risk, because a single regional event stops all the tier two supply and therefore the tier one supplier','Currency risk only, arising from the regional exchange rate','C','Diversification at tier two is illusory when every alternative shares the same geography and therefore the same hazards. Mapping beyond tier one is the only way this exposure becomes visible.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Planning and Execution','exam','How does the master production schedule differ from the forecast?','It covers a longer planning horizon','It is a build plan constrained by capacity and material, expressed in specific end items and dates','It is produced by the sales function rather than operations','It is stated at product family level rather than item level','B','The forecast is an estimate of demand; the master production schedule is a commitment to build, tested against available capacity and material. Confusing the two produces a schedule that cannot be executed.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Improvement and Best Practices','exam','When is benchmarking most useful?','When the processes being compared have the same scope and definitions, so the gap that appears is real rather than definitional','When the comparator chosen is the recognised market leader','When the comparison data is publicly available','When the exercise is repeated on the same date each year','A','A benchmark only supports action if the two processes start and stop at the same points and count the same things. A gap against a leading company measured differently produces a target that cannot be reached or explained.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Design','hard','Adding a supplier in a second country raises unit cost by four per cent on the thirty per cent of a AED 10 million spend that moves, and reduces expected annual disruption loss from AED 900,000 to AED 200,000. What is the correct decision?','Reject it, because unit cost must not increase','Reject it, because expected disruption loss is not a real cost','Accept it, because the AED 700,000 reduction in expected loss far exceeds the AED 120,000 of additional unit cost','Defer the decision until a disruption actually occurs','C','The added cost is four per cent of AED 3 million, about AED 120,000, against an expected benefit of AED 700,000. Expected loss is a real cost even in years when the event does not occur, which is why it must be modelled before the event.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Planning and Execution','exam','What does collaborative planning, forecasting and replenishment require above all else?','A common software platform between the parties','A long term contract with committed volumes','Trading partners of comparable size','Shared demand data and an agreed exception process for resolving differences between the two forecasts','D','The mechanism is the joint forecast and the rules for handling disagreement about it. Software and contracts support the process but do not create the shared view that makes it work.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Improvement and Best Practices','exam','Which pair of measures best describes customer service performance in a distribution business?','On-time in-full performance and order cycle time','Inventory turns and gross margin','Fill rate and warehouse headcount','Freight cost per unit and warehouse cost per unit','A','Customers experience whether the order arrived complete and on time, and how long it took. The other pairs describe internal efficiency and cost rather than the service delivered.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Design','exam','What must a reverse logistics design decide first?','Which carrier will collect the returned goods','What refund policy will apply to customers','The disposition rules for returned items, that is repair, refurbish, recycle or scrap, and where each is performed economically','How long the warranty period should be','C','The disposition decision determines the network, the transport flows and the recovery value of the whole reverse chain. Carrier, refund and warranty decisions follow from it.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Planning and Execution','hard','Capacity requirements planning shows a work centre overloaded in week six only, with spare capacity in weeks five and seven. What is the least disruptive response?','Purchase an additional machine for that work centre','Cancel or defer customer orders in week six','Outsource that product family permanently','Level the load across weeks five to seven, or add a shift in week six','D','A single period overload surrounded by spare capacity is a scheduling problem rather than a capacity problem. Capital, order cancellation and outsourcing are permanent answers to a temporary imbalance.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Improvement and Best Practices','exam','Which is a valid sustainability measure for a supply chain?','The number of suppliers describing themselves as environmentally responsible','Emissions per unit shipped, measured against a defined scope and boundary','Recycled content claimed by suppliers in their marketing material','The existence of a published environmental policy','B','A measure must be quantified per unit of activity and bounded, or improvement cannot be distinguished from a change in volume or in what is counted. Counts of claims and policies record intent rather than performance.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Design','exam','What is the decoupling point in a supply chain?','The point at which ownership of the goods transfers','The point at which the customer pays for the goods','The point at which order-driven activity meets forecast-driven activity, and where strategic inventory is held','The point at which the transport mode changes','C','Everything upstream of the decoupling point is made to forecast and everything downstream is made to order, which is why the strategic stock sits there. Moving that point is the main lever on both responsiveness and inventory.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Planning and Execution','exam','What does a rolling twelve month plan with a four week frozen zone give the supplier?','A guarantee of the price for twelve months','A guarantee of the volume for twelve months','Nothing beyond information already available','Stable near term demand it can resource, together with visibility of the longer horizon for capacity planning','D','The frozen zone converts the near horizon into something that can actually be scheduled and bought against. The rolling horizon behind it supports capacity decisions without committing the buyer to the volumes.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Improvement and Best Practices','hard','A continuous improvement programme reports substantial savings that never appear in the financial accounts. What is the most likely cause?','The capacity or cost released was never removed from the budget or redeployed, so the benefit was created but not captured','The measurement of the improvement was technically incorrect','The improvement projects did not actually deliver any change','Inflation absorbed the savings before they could be booked','A','Process improvement releases capacity, and capacity that is neither removed nor filled with new work simply becomes slack. Capturing the benefit requires an explicit decision about what happens to what was freed.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Design','exam','What does supply chain segmentation mean?','Dividing the warehouse into separate storage zones','Serving different product and customer combinations with different supply chain designs and service policies','Grouping suppliers by the region they operate in','Separating the inbound and outbound logistics teams','B','Segmentation recognises that one design cannot be optimal for products and customers with different demand and service profiles. It applies the responsive or efficient choice at segment level rather than company level.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Planning and Execution','exam','What is the purpose of cycle counting?','To satisfy the external auditor without a full annual count','To reduce the level of inventory held','To speed up the picking process','To find and remove the causes of record inaccuracy continuously, so that the plan can be trusted','D','Cycle counting is a process control that surfaces the errors that make planning unreliable, not merely a counting method. Correcting records without pursuing the cause guarantees the same discrepancy returns.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Improvement and Best Practices','exam','What must a supplier quality agreement define?','The specification, the inspection and acceptance regime, change control and the corrective action process','The unit price and the volume commitment','The payment terms and the invoicing process','The delivery terms and the nominated carrier','A','A quality agreement settles how conformity is judged, how changes are controlled and what happens when something fails. Commercial and logistics terms belong in the supply agreement itself.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Design','hard','Why can duty rates and rules of origin change the answer to a sourcing decision?','They alter the freight cost of the shipment','They alter the transit time of the shipment','They alter landed cost materially, and preferential treatment can make a nominally dearer origin the cheaper one delivered','They alter the technical specification of the goods','C','Duty is applied to the customs value and can exceed the price difference between two origins. A source that qualifies for preferential treatment under an agreement can therefore beat a cheaper origin that does not.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Planning and Execution','exam','A fast moving item is out of stock on the shelf although the system shows quantity available. What should be done first?','Place an emergency replenishment order with the supplier','Carry out a targeted physical count to test for record inaccuracy or misplaced stock before reordering','Re-run the forecast for the item','Check whether the selling price was recently changed','B','Ordering against a record that may be wrong either wastes money or leaves the real problem in place. A targeted count settles whether the issue is inventory accuracy or genuine supply.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Improvement and Best Practices','exam','Quality escapes rise at the same time across several unrelated suppliers. What should be investigated first?','Replacing the worst performing supplier','A common cause on the buyer side, such as a specification change, a drawing revision or a change to the receiving process','Increasing incoming inspection across all suppliers','Raising the penalties in each supplier contract','B','Simultaneous deterioration across independent suppliers points to a shared input rather than to coincidence. The shared input is almost always something the buyer changed.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Design','exam','How is risk in a global supply chain most effectively reduced?','By extending contract terms with key suppliers','By increasing inventory at every stage of the chain','By adding a second supplier for every item purchased','By mapping the chain to identify single points of failure and treating those specifically','D','Blanket measures spend money uniformly on a risk that is concentrated in a few nodes. Mapping shows where the chain actually breaks, so the mitigation can be targeted and affordable.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Planning and Execution','hard','Demand is 500 units a month, the cost of placing an order is AED 100, the unit cost is AED 50 and the annual holding rate is 20 per cent of unit cost. What is the economic order quantity?','About 155 units','About 245 units','About 346 units','About 490 units','C','Annual demand is 6,000 units and the annual holding cost per unit is AED 10, so the quantity is the square root of two times 6,000 times 100 divided by 10. That is the square root of 120,000, about 346 units.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Improvement and Best Practices','exam','Which suppliers should a supplier development programme target?','Suppliers that are strategically important and where a capability gap is measurable and can realistically be closed','The suppliers with the largest annual spend','The suppliers with the worst delivery performance','The suppliers most recently added to the supply base','A','Development is expensive, so it must be aimed where the improvement matters strategically and where the supplier can actually change. Spend, poor performance and newness do not by themselves indicate either condition.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Design','exam','What is the strongest reason to hold regional stock in a UAE free zone rather than on the mainland?','Import duty is deferred until the goods enter the mainland market, which improves cash flow for regional re-export','Inventory held in a free zone is cheaper to insure','Storage in a free zone is provided without charge','No customs documentation is required for free zone stock','A','Deferring the duty point until the goods are actually imported avoids paying duty on stock that will be re-exported. The other statements are not true of free zone operations.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Planning and Execution','exam','What should order management measure in a request for quotation marketplace?','The total number of suppliers registered on the platform','Quotation to award conversion and the time from request issue to quotations received, reported by category','The number of visits to the website','The number of buyers who have created an account','B','Conversion and response time describe whether the marketplace actually turns a request into a usable award. Registration and traffic counts measure reach rather than performance.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Improvement and Best Practices','hard','A supplier improvement project lifts on-time delivery from 85 per cent to 96 per cent, but the buyer inventory has not fallen. What is the most likely reason?','The on-time measure is being calculated incorrectly','Underlying demand rose and absorbed the improvement','The supplier is now holding the stock on the buyer behalf','The safety stock parameters were never recalculated to reflect the lower supply variability','D','Safety stock is set from assumed variability, and it does not fall on its own when the real variability improves. The benefit is only realised when the planning parameters are updated.'),
('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)','Supply Chain Design','exam','What is the design purpose of a supplier code of conduct backed by an audit regime?','To support the marketing of the company as a responsible business','To satisfy the requirements of a certification body','To reduce the probability and the impact of compliance, labour and continuity failures in the extended supply chain','To reduce the prices paid to suppliers','C','The code and the audits are risk controls applied to parts of the chain the buyer does not operate. Reputation and certification are consequences rather than the design objective.')
) as v(certification, syllabus_area, difficulty, question, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, rationale)
where not exists (select 1 from public.certification_exam_question where certification = 'CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)');

-- Proof: every bank in this batch must hold at least 40 questions on production.
do $$
declare r record; bad text := '';
begin
  for r in
    select c.name, coalesce(q.n, 0) as n
    from (values
      ('CIPS Level 4 Diploma in Procurement and Supply'),
      ('CIPS Level 5 Advanced Diploma in Procurement and Supply'),
      ('MCIPS (CIPS Level 6 Professional Diploma)'),
      ('CPSM (Certified Professional in Supply Management)'),
      ('CSCP (Certified Supply Chain Professional)')
    ) as c(name)
    left join (
      select certification, count(*) as n
      from public.certification_exam_question
      group by certification
    ) q on q.certification = c.name
  loop
    if r.n < 40 then
      bad := bad || format('%s has %s questions; ', r.name, r.n);
    end if;
  end loop;
  if bad <> '' then
    raise exception 'certifications_procurement proof failed: %', bad;
  end if;
end $$;
